  "description": "Assign grammatical part-of-speech tags based on empirical training frequencies with robust unigram fallback.\n\nMost words exhibit strong unigram POS priors; simple frequency-based lookup forms a strong initial baseline for syntactic tagging.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\n\\hat{t}_w = \\arg\\max_t C(w, t)\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Record co-occurrence frequency counts between words and grammatical POS tags\n2. Select most frequent historical tag for recognized vocabulary words\n3. Assign fallback tag to out-of-vocabulary test tokens\n\n### Returns\nList of predicted POS tag strings.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: POS Tagging\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nNatural language consists of discrete words and symbols arranged in sequential order. To process text with neural networks, we convert words into continuous embedding vectors and model their grammatical and semantic relationships. **POS Tagging** is a core sequence-processing mechanism used in modern Transformer and LLM architectures.\n\nMost words exhibit strong unigram POS priors; simple frequency-based lookup forms a strong initial baseline for syntactic tagging. In this problem, the goal is to: assign grammatical part-of-speech tags based on empirical training frequencies with robust unigram fallback.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\n\\hat{t}_w = \\arg\\max_t C(w, t)\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Record co-occurrence frequency counts between words and grammatical POS tags\n2. **Phase 2**: Select most frequent historical tag for recognized vocabulary words\n3. **Phase 3**: Assign fallback tag to out-of-vocabulary test tokens\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Self-Attention Mechanics**: Scaled dot-product attention computes all-to-all token interactions dynamically, allowing tokens to gather context from the entire sentence simultaneously.\n* **Position Encodings**: Because self-attention is permutation-invariant, positional encodings (like sinusoidal or Rotary Position Embeddings) inject word order into the representations.\n* **Vocabulary & Subwords**: Algorithms like BPE tokenize text into frequent subwords, allowing models to represent rare words without requiring an infinite vocabulary.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: train_tokens = [\"The\", \"dog\", \"runs\", \"fast\"], train_tags = [\"DET\", \"NOUN\", \"VERB\", \"ADV\"], test_tokens = [\"The\", \"cat\", \"runs\"], fallback_tag = \"NOUN\"\n\nOutput: [\"DET\", \"NOUN\", \"VERB\"]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated nlp_pos_tagging under standard test conditions."
